UA = UB = 0 (Enormal model). The effective mobility is given by μeff = U0 / (1 + UA·Eeff + UB·Eeff²), 
where Eeff is the vertical field in the channel. UA and UB describe mobility degradation from surface-roughness
and phonon scattering at the Si/SiO2 interface, which is what the Enormal model captures in TCAD.
Setting UA = UB = 0 removes this degradation, so μeff = U0 at all gate voltages. 
The current increases only slightly, and the difference appears at high gate voltage where the vertical field is large. 
Below threshold the vertical field is small, so the curves nearly coincide.

VSAT × 100 (HighFieldSaturation model). At a channel length of 65 nm, the lateral field is far above the critical field,
so carriers move at the saturation velocity vsat instead of at μE. This is the effect modelled by HighFieldSaturation in TCAD. 
Multiplying VSAT by 100 effectively switches velocity saturation off, so the carriers are no longer velocity-limited. 
This gives the largest increase in on-current, and the curve rises much more steeply with VG than the normal case.

